  "query_preview": "..\n\nUpstream of a trough to the west, flow aloft remains southerly \nover southern Arizona today. Early morning satellite imagery\nshows expansive cloud cover over much of the area as this\nsoutherly flow pushes moisture in the mid-levels poleward.\nOvernight rain has been kept almost entirely to the east on the\nNew Mexico side where more favorable upper level forcing sits. \nThough rain has remained to the east so far, precipitable water \nvalues continue to increase this morning with values of 1-1.25 \ninches over southeastern Arizona. An embedded shortwave in the \nparent trough moving over the Arizona/New Mexico border today \nwill add some extra synoptic forcing to the mix, however the \nexpansive cloud cover makes coverage of today's thunderstorm \ncoverage uncertain again. Just like yesterday, recent high- \nresolution models continue to show convective development \noccurring from the mid-afternoon through the early evening, this\ntime however forecast soundings look a bit more favorable than \nyesterday in regards to instability potential. Forecast \nshower/thunderstorm chances today are in the 20-40 percent range\nacross southeastern Arizona as the added upper level forcing \ngives a chance to just about any location in the area, even with\nthe cloud cover wrinkle. If stronger instability can develop, a\nflash flooding threat may arise with any thunderstorm today. \nOvernight showers and thunderstorms will be possible, though \nconfidence at any one location is low.\n\nThursday into Friday the western trough begins to open and move\nthrough the region before it finally exits to the northeast.\nAdditional shower/thunderstorm chances are forecast in the\nafternoon and evening, though guidance is still inconsistent\nwith coverage. Precipitable water will drop below an inch \nFriday and Saturday behind the exiting trough and the forecast \nis mostly dry.\n\nA slight chance of showers and thunderstorms returns on Sunday\nahead of an incoming upper low arriving from central California,\nwith greater chances Monday as the low crosses to the north. The\nslow approach of Hurricane Polo may add some moisture to the\nsetup Monday into Tuesday, however there is high uncertainty in\nhow the storm tracks beyond early next week. Divergence in\nguidance increases heavily once the forecast storm track reaches\nas far north as Cabo, so details will need to be worked out in\nthe coming days.",
